// Conformance row K37 — dispatching to a SUSPENDING conformance body through
// `any Trait` is refused, not silently run to completion
//
// A frame is a compile-time identity: the caller embeds the callee's frame BY
// VALUE, so it has to know at compile time which body it is embedding. Dynamic
// dispatch has no such identity — the concrete type behind `&any Greeter` is a
// vtable word — so there is nothing to embed and no design that says what
// should be. What the compiler did instead was worse than refusing: the
// existential dispatch was recorded as a merely-CONSERVATIVE suspension
// source (the same treatment a call through a closure gets), no frame was
// built anywhere in the program, and `yield_now()` inside the conformance body
// ran outside any frame — where it is a no-op. The cooperative contract was
// simply not applied, with no diagnostic.
//
// Design 223's ruling: work where the mechanism exists, refuse cleanly where
// it does not. This is the second half. The refusal names the design that is
// owed, and it is ANCHORED at the dispatch the author wrote.
//
// AND THE REFUSAL IS PENDING, NOT PERMANENT (design 275 §3 ruling 2, user,
// Sep 18). The design that is owed has a carrier: SL-322, a heap-allocated
// frame with an indirect resume. Suspending RECURSION needs the same runtime
// shape, so the two land together and neither alone — which is why the hint
// names SL-322 beside DF-223b, and why `examples/errors/coro_suspending_recursion.saw`
// asserts the same citation from the other side. `coro_shapes.PENDING_SL322`
// is the row both messages render their pending note from, so the table, the
// message and this file cannot say three different things.
//
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: dispatch through `any Greeter`
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: DF-223b
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: SL-322

import std.task.*

trait Greeter {
    func greet(&self) -> String
}

struct Person { n: String }

extension Person: Greeter {
    func greet(&self) -> String {
        yield_now()
        self.n
    }
}

func shout(g: &any Greeter) -> String {
    g.greet()
}

func main() {
    let p = Person(n: "ada")
    let s = shout(&p)
    print("{s}")
}
